This is the vi tutorial.  Send comments to ach@pucc-h

When you reach the bottom of the screen, type control-F or control-D
to advance to the next page.  If your screen is half-empty, control-D
is proabably the best way to go.  More about these commands later.

The purpose of this document is to teach a beginner how to use the 'vi' 
editor.  Unless specifically stated otherwise, in the rest of this 
tutorial:
	1) ^<char> stands for "control-<char>" (hold the 'control' key down,
		then depress the <char> key.
	2) when it says type "foo", do NOT type the quotation marks unless
		specifically told to.
	3) when the characters ">>" are seen next to the left margin, the
		paragraph contains directions for you to follow.

And now, on with the lesson! If you need to exit this tutorial, type ":q!".
The command should be echoed on the last line of the screen.  If this doesn't
work, type <escape> or <rubout> and try again.  If you ever mess up the file
so badly that you can't understand it anymore, try "u" or ":e!".  As a last
resort, quit and restart the tutorial.


Adjusting the Screen
--------- --- ------

The first thing to learn is how to adjust the "window" into your file.  You
may have just learned how to move forward a page with ^F.  To move backwards,
the command is ^B.  Note that these commands can be easily remembered by the
mnemonics "Forward screen" and "Backwards screen".

>>  Try typing a "^B" now to back you up a page, and then "^F" to return
	you here.  Note that a few lines near the top or bottom of the
	page appeared on both screenfuls.  This gives a bit of "continuity"
	when paging through a file.

There are a few other commands that adjust the screen.  A "^D" goes forward
about half a screenful, while "^U" scrolls down.  These commands are useful
when you want to see something that is past the top or bottom of the screen
by a little bit.  Mnemonically, you can think of them as "scroll Down" and
"scroll Up".  Another pair of commands scroll the screen by just one line
in either direction.  To get one line at the bottom, type "^E" (Expose a
line), and to get one at the top, "^Y" (no mnemonic for this one, sorry!).
On some terminals without an insert line feature, a ^Y acts like an ^U.

>>  Use the commands just described to place this line at the top of the
	screen.

There are two more commands available that operate on the screen.  These
are the retype ("^L") and redraw ("^R") commands.  If your screen should be
garbled due to output from something else or glitches in the line, a "^L"
should clean things up.  If it doesn't, then the garbage really is in your
file, probably due to an error of yours. The "^R" command will be discussed
later.

>>  Try the retype command now.  You should see the screen clear, followed
	by a retyping of the screen.  If your terminal outputs quickly, all
	you might see could be just a blink.

Summary
-------

These commands adjust the screen:

	^F	Forward one screen
	^B	Backwards one screen
	^D	Scroll down one-half screen
	^U	Scroll up one-half screen
	^E	Expose one more line at the bottom
	^Y	Expose one more line at the top
	^L	Retype the screen
	^R	Eliminate "@" lines (to be explained later)


Basic Cursor Control
----- ------ -------

Now you know how to manipulate the screen, but how do you move around in
it?  One way is to use the cursor positioning commands.  If you like to
play Rogue, then the commands are very easy to remember: "h" goes to the
left, "j" down, "k" up, and "l" forward.  The easiest way to remember these
is that "h" is leftmost and "l" is rightmost of the four keys, and that
a control-J is a linefeed, which is like what the "j" command does.

>>  Try these commands now to position the cursor on the period at the end
	of this sentence.

If your terminal has arrow keys, then you may be able to use them instead
of the "hjkl" keys.  These commands are used so often that there are other
synonyms for them.  A <space> is identical to "l", which makes sense since
it effectively looks like a space.  A "^H" is the same as "h", and "^J" the
same as "j" for the same reasons.  More mnemonically, "^P" acts like "k"
and goes up one line (Previous line) and "^N" acts like "j" (Next line).

>>  Position the cursor in the middle of some line, and then use the up and
	down cursor commands to move it over a blank line.  What happens?

Note that these commands do not necessarily move the cursor in the right
direction all the time.  If you are on a line and move to one that is
shorter, then the cursor is placed at the end of the shorter line.  If you
then immediately move to a longer line, then the cursor will be placed in
the same column as the original line.  However, if you do something else on
the shorter line, then if you move up or down, your position on that line
will be used as the column to move to.

>>  Go to the beginning of a line and try to move left.  Now go to the end
	of a line and try to move right.  The bell should ring when you do
	this.

You cannot use the left and right cursor commands to go past the beginning
or end of a line.

There are two more line-positioning commands that you should be aware of.
A <carriage return> (CR for short) or a "+" goes to the next line, but also
positions the cursor at the first non-white character in the line.  A "-"
does the same, but goes backward a line instead.  The "+" and "-" commands
are mnemonics in themselves.

>>  Position the cursor in the middle of a line and then try either the "+"
	or "-" command and see where it puts you.  Then, move the cursor to
	the middle of the ">> . . ." line above and try the "+" command.
	See where it puts you?

Summary
-------

The basic cursor motion commands are:

	h		Cursor left
	^H	   	   "    "
	<left-arrow>       "    "

	j		Cursor down
	^J	   	   "    "
	^N	   	   "    "
	<down-arrow>       "    "

	k		Cursor up
	^P	   	   "    "
	<up-arrow>	   "    "

	l		Cursor right
	<space>    	   "     "
	<right-arrow>	   "     "

	CR		Next line, at first non-white character
	+		 "     "   "    "    "    "       "
	-		Previous line, at first non-white character


Advanced Cursor Motion
-------- ------ ------

There are other commands that pertain to cursor motion that move farther
than one character or one line.  These will be the topic of this section.

Often, it is desirable to go to the beginning or end of a line.  Vi has
commands that do this.  A "0" (zero, not capital-o) goes to the first
position in a line.  "^" (an ASCII caret, not the up-arrow key) goes to the
first non-white character in the current line.  A "$" goes to the end of
the line (this one is derived from the symbol used in regular
expressions--if you don't know what a regular expression is, don't worry).

>>  Try these commands now.
	This line is available to show you the difference between "0" and
	"^".

Sometimes you know you want to go to a specific line number or column.
There are two commands that allow you to do this, but before you learn
them, you must learn the idea of a numeric argument.  

Almost any vi command can be preceded by a number.  Most commands take this
number to be a repeat count, that is, "10k" does "k" 10 times.  However,
some do not.  An example of ones that don't are "^D" and "^U".  An argument
given to these specifies the number of lines to scroll the screen, and that
argument will be used in the future whenever you say "^D" or "^U" without
an argument.

Now, back to moving the cursor.  If you want to go to a specific line in
the file, the command is "<line number>G", where <line number> is the
line number in decimal, with the first line in the file being line one.
Likewise, to go to a specific column, the command is "<column number>|".

>>  This is line 182.  Use the "G" command to get here and then move to
	the tenth column in the line.

There are also ways of positioning the cursor with respect to the screen.
The "H" (Home) command puts the cursor in the upper left corner of the
screen, "M" (Middle) puts you at the beginning of the line in the middle
of the screen, and "L" (Last) puts you on the last line.  Arguments to 
"H" and "L" put you on the nth line from the top or bottom.  "M" ignores
arguments.

>>  Try these commands now.

Now we finally get to the most useful cursor motion commands.  These act on
text objects like words and sentences. First, word motion commands.  To go
forward to the next beginning of a word, type "w" (Word).  To go backwards,
type "b" (Backwards word).  And to go forward to the next end of a word, the
command is "e" (End of Word).  A word is delimited by blanks and punctuation.
If you want to move over words that have punctuation in them, like a hyphen,
or words that have, for example, a comma or a period following them, the 
capitalized versions of the above commands ("W", "B", and "E") consider only
blank-delimited words.  These commands are able to change the line that the
cursor is on, unlike the single character commands ("hjkl").

>>  Try the word motion commands now.  Then move down to this line.
	Here-is-a-word-with-punctuation.  See how the capitalized commands
	differ from the lower-case ones.

Far less used are commands that move by sentences, paragraphs, and sections.
They are:  "(" for backwards sentence, ")" for forward sentence; "{" 
backwards paragraph, "}" forward paragraph; and "[[" backwards section, "]]"
forwards section.  A sentence is delimited by one of ".?!" followed by two
spaces.  A paragraph begins after a blank line or at the beginning of 
an nroff paragraph macro command (if you don't know about nroff, don't 
concern yourself with it here).  A section begins with an nroff macro, or
if the file is "C" source code, at the "{" that begins a function block.

>>  You can try the sentence and paragraph motion commands here.  There are
	no sections in this file, so those commands will simply take you
	to the beginning and end of the file.

Summary
-------

The advanced cursor motion commands are:

	0	to the first character in the line
	^	to the first non-white character
	$	to the end of the line

	<n>G	to the nth line in the file
	<n>|	to the nth column in the line

	H	to home position on screen (upper-left corner)
	M	to middle line on screen
	L	to last line on screen

	w	forward to next beginning of a word
	W	   "     "   "      "      " " blank-delimited word
	b	backward to next beginning of a word
	B	   "     "   "       "     "  " blank-delimited word
	e	forward to next end of a word
	E	   "     "   "   "  "  " blank-delimited word

	(	backwards to next beginning of a sentence
	)	forwards  "   "       "     "  "    "
	{	backwards to next beginning of a paragraph
	}	forwards  "   "       "     "  "    "
	[[	backwards to next beginning of a section/function
	]]	forwards  "   "       "     "  "    "       "


Searching
---------

Often you do not know exactly what line in the file you want to go, and you
don't feel line using "^F" and "^B" to page through it all.  However, it is 
usually the case that you have an idea as to what is nearby what you are 
looking for.  For example, suppose you wanted to find this page quickly.
You probably don't know that this is about 250 lines into the file, so "G"
doesn't help you.  However, you can probably remember that the word 
"Searching" begins this section.  So the question is:  How do you find it?
This is vi's search commands come into play.  To initiate a search from
the current cursor position forward, type "/".  A "/" will appear on the 
last line of the screen.  Now any more characters you type will appear after
the "/".  When you type an "<esc>" or a CR, vi will begin the search, and 
put the cursor on the first character of the search string, or, if the
string is nowhere in the file, will ring the bell and tell you.  If you
want to search from your cursor backwards in the file, instead of "/",
the command is "?".

>>  Use the search commands to place the cursor on the angle brackets at 
	the beginning of the line.

Every time you search for something, vi remembers the search string so that
it is available for you the next time you search.  If you type "/<esc>" or
"?<esc>" without specifying a search string, vi uses the previous string.
Another way of repeating a search is to use the "n" command.  It will 
search forward if the previous search command was "/", or backwards if 
it was "?".  "N" will reverse the sense of the last search, i.e., backwards
if it was forwards, and vice versa.

>>  Adjust the screen so that both this and the last ">>" instruction fit
	on it, then use "H" to go to the home position.  Then search forward
	for the ">>", and repeat this a couple of times, once using "/<esc>"
	and also with "n", and then try "N".  If you go far away from this
	page, You can look for the word "SEARCH" and it will bring you back
	here.

These searches are actually quite powerful, since you can specify more
than just a normal string.  Actually, the search uses what is called a 
regular expression, like the ones taught by the "learn editor" program.
You should take some time acquainting yourself with this, since this means
that certain characters are special to the search, and might not do quite
as expected.  Quickly ".", "*", "^", and "$" do not act as normal characters.
Precede them with a \ if you want to search for one of them.

There is also a quick one-character search that works on the current line
only.  Typing "f<x>" where <x> is any character, places the cursor on the 
next <x> in the current line, while "F<x>" does the same, but backwards.
A different flavor of this search is "t<x>" and "T<x>" which moves the 
cursor up 't'o <x>, instead of on it.  When you do a character search, you
do not affect the string remembered by vi for "/" and "?" searches.  To
quickly repeat a character search, the command is ";", while "," will
reverse the direction of the last character search.

>>  Play around with these character searches on this line.  Try looking
	for e's, since there are quite a few.

Summary
-------

	/<string>	search forwards for regular expression "<string>"
	?<string>	search backwards for regular expression "<string>"
	n		repeat last reg. ex. search ("/" or "?")
	N		repeat last reg. ex. search, but in opposite direction

	f<x>		find character <x> forward, and place cursor on it
	F<x>		find character <x> backwards, and place cursor on it
	t<x>		like "f", but place cursor before <x>
	T<x>		like "F", but place cursor after <x>
	;		repeat last character search
	,		reverse direction of last character search


Inserting
---------

So, now you know how to move around in the file, but not yet how to add 
anything to it.  This is the first section where you'll be asked to modify
this file.  Don't worry about destroying it, since you won't be affecting
the master copy of it.  If you end up totally botching it, just exit (with
":q!") and try again.

The hardest thing to remember about inserting is where the inserted text
will go. Also, depending on how "smart" your terminal is, it may appear
as if the text you are inserting is overwriting the existing text.  This
is not so.  When you end the insert, the screen will be properly updated. 
This is to minimize the amount of screen updating the program has to do
on "dumb" terminals.

The two basic insert commands are "i" and "a".  To start inserting, you type
one of these, and then the text you want to insert.  To leave insert mode,
you then type an <esc>.  The difference between these two is that "i" Inserts
this text before the cursor, and "a" Appends it after the cursor. 

>>  To better see how this works, try them now.  Move the cursor so that
	it is somewhere that you'll remember, type either "i" or "a", some
	text that you want to insert, and then an <esc> (Remember, that's
	the escape key, not the characters "<esc>").

While you are in insert mode, none of the commands described so far work.
To do something else, like scroll backwards a page, it is necessary to
first leave insert mode with an <esc>, do the desired command (like ^B),
and then re-enter insert mode.  There are a few commands, however, that
do work.  Since it is very possible that you will mistype something, a
few commands let you erase what you have just typed.  A "^H" or your
erase character erases the previous character, a "^W" erases the previous
word, and your kill character (usually "^X") erases the current line.
However, note that these do NOT let you erase something that you have 
not typed since you last entered insert mode.  That is,  if you typed
"Fourscore and seven ", left insert mode to do something, and then returned
to complete the sentence, once you enter insert mode, you cannot delete 
the previous characters unless you leave insert mode again. 

>>  See how this works now.  Insert some characters, then use one of the
	above to erase them.  Try erasing more than you have input and
	see what happens.  Also, note that characters you erase remain 
	on the screen until you leave insert mode.

What if you want to type a "^W", for example, into your file?  There are
two commands that let you quote the next character so that it is taken
to be input text even if insert mode usually takes it to be a command.
They are "\" and "^V".  If you type a "\" and the next character is 
an insert mode command, then the "\" will be replaced by the character.
However, if it isn't then the "\" and the next character you type will 
both be inserted into the file.  On the other hand, "^V" always quotes
the next character, whether or not it is special.  When you type a "^V"
a "^" will appear at the current cursor position.  The next character
you type will replace it.

>>  Try these now to see how they differ.  In insert mode, type "\^F\a"
	and then "^V^F^Va" and see the difference.

There are other flavors of inserting also.  To append to the current line,
the command is "A", while to insert before the first non-blank in the line,
the command is "I".  An "o" will open a line below the current one, and
put you in insert mode, while an "O" will do the same, except that the line
is opened above.

>>  Try adding another ">" at the beginning of this line, and another
	period at the end of this one. 
	Then add some lines above and below this one.

There are two other commands which are related to the insert commands,
but different in that they replace the current text rather than inserting
some.  To replace the single character that the cursor is on, type "r<x>"
where "<x>" is the character you want to replace it with.  To enter a 
"replace" mode that is identical to insert mode, except that characters
are overwritten instead, type "R".  You leave this mode by typing an
<esc> just as before.

>>  I have mestyped a word on this line.  Correct it, and then change
	this word "foo" to be "bar".

Summary
-------

The insert commands are:

	i	enter insert mode, insert before cursor
	a	enter insert mode, append after cursor
	I	insert before first non-blank in line
	A	append to end of line
	o	open line below and enter insert mode
	O	open line above
	r<x>	replace character under cursor with <x>
	R	enter insert mode, except that characters are replaced
			rather than inserted

The insert mode commands are:

	^H	erase last character
	erase	(your erase character) same as ^H
	^W	erase last word
	kill	(your kill character) erase this insert line
	\	quote next character if it is an insert mode command
	^V	quote next character
	<esc>	terminate insert mode
	intr	(your interrupt character) terminate insert mode abnormally


Deleting and Changing Text
-------- --- -------- ----

Fine, now you know how to insert the text that you want.  But, unless
you're perfect, you will probably make some mistakes.  The following
commands will tell you how to fix them.  To delete the character that 
your cursor is currently on, type an 'x'.  This is like "x'ing" out
the characters you don't want.  Likewise, an 'X' will delete the character
immediately before the cursor.  These two commands are for small changes.
If you want to delete more than just characters, the 'd' command will
do it for you.  This command is unlike the ones you have already encountered.
It is called an "operator".  What this means is that typing 'd' will do
nothing until you type one more command, which is how you control what to
delete.  The "one more command" is a motion command of some sort, either
a horizontal cursor motion or a search.  The 'd' will take this com mand,
and delete all characters between the cursor and the location that you fi-
nally end up on.  For example, 'd5l' deletes 5 characters from the cursor
forward, since '5l' would have put you 5 characters ahead of your current
position.  Likewise, 'dw' will delete the next word and 'dfz' will delete
between where you were and the next "z" on the line.  As a special case,
doubling the delete command, 'dd' deletes an entire line, and 'D' acts like
'd$', that is, it deletes to the end of the line.  On terminals without the
capability to delete a line, instead of redrawing the screen correctly,
it will place a "@" next to the left margin.  This is where the "^R"
command mentioned earlier comes into play.  It will force vi to eliminate
those "@" lines.

>>  Ooooops, I seem seem to have made have made some misteaks on
	this line.  Fix them.
	this line.  Fix them.

Often you want to just change a certain amount of text.  For example, suppose
you had typed "foo" instead of "bar".  Of course, you could delete the word
and then enter insert mode, but the change operator 'c' does it all for you.
Like the delete operator, it takes a motion command after it to tell it 
what to change.  It then replaces the last character that would be replaced
on your screen with a "$", and you are placed in insert mode to make the
change which you end with an <esc> like a normal insert.  Like the delete
operator, 'cc' changes an entire line, and 'C' changes to the end of the
line.  There is a synonym for the 'cc' command called 'S' which does same.
The substitute command 's' changes only the character the cursor is
currently on.  Note that this is not just a replace character command, since
you can substitute a word for the character, for example.  

>>  Play around with these commands a little and change some words and
	lines.

Summary
-------

These operators delete and change text:  (double to affect lines)

	d	delete
	c	change

These commands delete and change text:

	x	delete character at current position
	X	delete character before cursor
	C	change rest of line
	S	substitute all of current line
	D	delete to end of line
	s	substitute characters


OOPS! (or "How to correct your mistakes")
-----  --  --- -- ------- ---- --------  

Sometimes you find that you have made a horrible mistake--like deleting
your entire file.  It is possible to restart your edit from the beginning,
as long as you haven't rewritten the file (we'll get to this a little later).
However, suppose you just spent 163 hours in the editor straight without
exitting.  Doing it all over again probably does not appeal to you.  You
might ask, "Isn't it possible to correct that 'one' little error?"  Luckily,
vi is not sadistic.

If the problem was that you deleted something that you didn't really want
to, there is a way to retrieve your last 9 deletes.  This is the 'put'
command, invoked with the 'p' or 'P' keys.  To undelete your last block
of text and put it before the cursor, use 'p', and after the cursor, 'P'.
If you want to restore your third previous delete, prepend the command
'"3' to either 'p' or 'P' (yes, this time the command is a double quote).

>> Try deleting and undeleting a few pieces of text.  In particular,
	try to understand where the "put" commands will place them.
	There is a difference between undeleting entire lines and
	parts of lines.

Of course, the problem could have been creating by something other than
a delete command.  Vi allows you to 'undo' the last command that changed
your file with the 'u' command.  This can very often be invaluable,
especially once you learn the more powerful commands that can change every
line in your file in one fell swoop.  Another type of undo command lets
you restore the line that you are currently on to what it was when you
first moved onto it.  This is done by 'U'.  However, remember that once
you change lines, you can't go back to it to restore it.

>> Change all sorts of things and try using the undo commands to fix them
	back up again.

Summary
-------

These commands undo changes:

	p	undelete after cursor
	P	undelete before cursor
	u	undo last change to file
	U	restore current line to initial form


File Manipulation
---- ------------

Strangely enough, vi really has really very few commands that affect
files.  The commands that work on files really belong to vi's cousin,
called "ex" which is a line oriented editor like "ed" or "xed", but more
powerful.  We will not mention this further, except to say that learning
ex at some time practically adds 50% more power to vi.  All of the
commands that are part of ex begin with a ':'.  After typing it, vi will
place a ":" on the last line of the screen, and your cursor after it. 
Any more characters that you type will appear there.  The command will
not start executing until you type a carriage return or an <ESC>.  

And now, on to the topic at hand.  The way to exit vi and write your
changes back to your file is to type either 'ZZ' or ':wq' (remember that
':' commands have to be terminated with a <CR> or <ESC>).  If you only
want to write the changes but not exit, type ':w' or ':w name' to write
the file to a file named "name".  If "name" already exists, you will
have to use ':w! name' instead.  Vi will warn you about this
automatically.  If you haven't made any changes and just want to exit,
type ':q'.  If you've made some changes, vi will warn you about quitting
without writing the file.  You will have to use ':q!' to quit in this
case.  This is useful if you've really botched this editting session
and would like to forget about the whole thing.  However, you should be
sure you want to do this, because all the changes that you made will be
lost for all eternity.  Another useful command is ':e'.  This one will
reread the file that you are editting.  If you've made some changes, vi
will tell you to use ':e!' instead if you really want to do this.  The
purpose of this command is like ':q!', that is, to try again if you've
damaged the file beyond all hope.  Finally, a very useful command is
':f' or '^G', which will tell you some information about the file that
you are editting.

>>  The problem with these commands is that most of them will cause you
	to exit this tutorial.  A safe one to try is writing this
	tutorial file to a file on your own directory.  Try both it and
	the '^G' command.

Summary
-------

These commands affect files:

	ZZ		write file and exit
	:wq		  "    "    "   "

	:w		write changes
	:w name 	write file to "name"
	:w! name	overwrite file "name"

	:e		re-edit file
	:e!		re-edit file, discarding changes

	:q		quit
	:q!		quit, discarding changes


The Final Word
--- ----- ----

I hope that you have all found this tutorial useful.  Please send any
comments to me at physics:crl.  I have not explained all the commands
that are available, but only what I have found I've used a lot.  Refer
to the manual for a more complete description.

Happy Hacking!!!
